To force Jenkins to use your spacious 17 GB free disk space partition instead of the small 956 MB RAM partition, you need to point Jenkins' temporary directory to a location inside your root (/) directory.
Here is exactly how to move it:

Step 1: Create a Temp Folder on your EC2 Instance

Connect to your SUSE EC2 terminal and create a dedicated folder inside the Jenkins home directory on your main drive:
bash
# 1. Create a new directory for temp files
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/jenkins/workspace_tmp

# 2. Grant full ownership permissions to the jenkins user
sudo chown -R jenkins:jenkins /var/lib/jenkins/workspace_tmp
Use code with caution.

Step 2: Point Jenkins to the New Directory

1. Go to Dashboard > Manage Jenkins > Nodes and Clouds.
2. Click the gear icon ⚙️ (Configure) on the far right of the Built-In Node row.
3. Scroll down to the Node Properties section.
4. Check the box next to Environment variables.
5. Click the Add button and fill in these values:
	• Name: JENKINS_JAVA_OPTS
	• Value: -Djava.io.tmpdir=/var/lib/jenkins/workspace_tmp

Step 3: Revert the Threshold Constraints

Since Jenkins will now be checking your main 17 GB storage drive, you can safely change your Free Temp Space Thresholds back to standard numbers so your server stays protected:
1. In that same window, look at your Disk Space Monitoring Thresholds section.
2. Change Free Temp Space Threshold back to 1GiB.
3. Change Free Temp Space Warning Threshold back to 2GiB.
4. Click the blue Save button at the bottom of the page.

Step 4: Restart the Jenkins Service

For Jenkins to register the new Java optimization settings (JENKINS_JAVA_OPTS), restart the service via your terminal:
bash
sudo systemctl restart jenkins
Use code with caution.
Once the service restarts, Jenkins will completely ignore the RAM-backed /tmp drive. It will look directly at your root drive partition, see 16.94 GiB of free temp space, and your builds will run flawlessly without ever tripping the safety threshold again!
